NETWORK
  x := a ^ b;
END_NETWORK
=== refused
NETWORK_UNKNOWN_OPERATOR is no operator of network text

#### old-backticked-literal — a literal is bare
=== text
IMPLEMENTATION FBD
NETWORK
  x := `5`;
END_NETWORK
=== refused
NETWORK_BAD_EXPRESSION a literal between backticks

#### old-spaced-minus — the minus touches its digit
=== text
IMPLEMENTATION FBD
NETWORK
  x := - 1;
END_NETWORK
=== refused
NETWORK_BAD_EXPRESSION a negative literal is the minus touching its digit

#### bad-escape — delimited text spells IEC's escapes only
=== text
IMPLEMENTATION LD
NETWORK
  // cost $5
  x := a;
END_NETWORK
=== refused
NETWORK_PARSE the escape '$5'

#### unclosed-backtick — backticked text runs to its closing backtick on its line
=== text
IMPLEMENTATION LD
NETWORK
  x := `a;
END_NETWORK
=== refused
NETWORK_PARSE an unclosed backtick

#### let-statement — `LET` is no statement: the item is the name before `:=`, and `LET g1` leaves it without its `;`
=== text
IMPLEMENTATION FBD
NETWORK
  LET g1 := a;
  x := g1;
END_NETWORK
=== refused
NETWORK_PARSE 'g1': an item ends with `;`.

#### header-number — a NETWORK header holds no number: it is NETWORK [LABEL: x] [DISABLED] [TITLE: "…"]
=== text
IMPLEMENTATION LD
NETWORK 0 LD
  x := a;
END_NETWORK
=== refused
NETWORK_PARSE '0' in a NETWORK header: the header is NETWORK [LABEL: x] [DISABLED] [TITLE: "…"], and it ends at its newline.
